    "type": "question",
    "label": "31",
    "prompt": "Let $P(a,b)$ lie on $y=f(x)$.\n\nFind the new coordinates of $P$ on $y=\\frac{f(x-1)+6}{4}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\left(a+1,\\frac{b+6}{4}\\right)$",
      "worked": "Since $f(a)=b$, the new point $(X,Y)$ satisfies\n$$\\begin{align*}X-1&=a\\\\X&=a+1\\\\Y&=\\frac{b+6}{4}\\end{align*}$$\nThe horizontal translation is right 1. Vertically, translate up 6 then dilate by factor $\\frac14$.\n\nThe new coordinates are $\\left(a+1,\\frac{b+6}{4}\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
